{
  "abstract": "We formalize two sharp logarithmic Kakeya upper bounds in every integer dimension n ≥ 2. For 0 < δ ≤\n1, the Kakeya maximal operator takes the largest average of |f| over open radius-δ neighborhoods of\nunit segments in each direction. Its norm from Lⁿ(ℝⁿ) to Lⁿ(Sⁿ⁻¹) is at most Cₙ[log(2/δ)]^((n−1)/n).\nThe tube estimate applies to any finite family of N unit directions with pairwise Euclidean distance\nat least δ, arbitrary centers, and nonnegative weights aⱼ. Let Tⱼ be the open radius-2δ neighborhood\nof the corresponding centered unit segment, let 1_T denote the indicator of T, and set q = n/(n−1).\nThe integral over ℝⁿ of (∑ⱼ aⱼ 1_{Tⱼ})^q is at most Cₙ log(2/δ) δ^(n−1) ∑ⱼ aⱼ^q. All constants\ndepend only on n. The same estimate holds for radius-δ tubes. Hölder's inequality gives tube-union\nvolume at least cₙNδ^(n−1)/[log(2/δ)]^(n−1), and a direction net gives volume at least\ncₙ/[log(2/δ)]^(n−1) for the δ-neighborhood of every Kakeya set. This matches the construction of\nFernández-Delgado and de la Salle. The estimates imply the Kakeya maximal function conjecture and\nfull Minkowski dimension for every bounded Kakeya set. Written lower-bound arguments establish\noptimality of the logarithmic powers. Only the two upper estimates are Lean theorems; the geometric\nconsequences and sharpness arguments are not formalized here. The accompanying abstract and summary\ngive the fully typeset statements.",
